**Setup and key observations.** $E = AC \cap BD$ is the intersection of diagonals. $\ell \ni E$ meets $AB$ at $P$, $BC$ at $Q$, so $P,E,Q$ are collinear. Circle $\omega$ is tangent to $\ell$ at $E$ (so $E \in \omega$) and passes through $D$. Since $E \in BD$, points $E,B,D$ are collinear. $R$ is the second intersection of $\omega$ and $\odot(O)$ (the first being $D$).

We use **directed angles mod $\pi$** throughout.

---

**Step 1: $A, P, E, R$ are concyclic.**

Since $\ell$ is tangent to $\omega$ at $E$, the tangent–chord angle with chord $ER$ gives:
$$\angle QER = \angle EDR. \tag{1}$$
(Both equal the angle in the alternate segment of $\omega$.)

Since $E, B, D$ are collinear (with $E$ on segment $BD$), ray $DE$ coincides with ray $DB$, so:
$$\angle EDR = \angle BDR. \tag{2}$$

Since $B, D, R, A$ all lie on $\odot(O)$, the inscribed angles $\angle BDR$ and $\angle BAR$ subtend the same arc $BR$:
$$\angle BDR = \angle BAR. \tag{3}$$

Since $A, P, B$ are collinear, $\angle BAR = \angle PAR$, so combining (1)–(3):
$$\angle QER = \angle PAR.$$
As $P, E, Q$ are collinear, $\angle QER = \angle PER$ (mod $\pi$). Thus:
$$\angle PER = \angle PAR.$$
These are directed angles subtending chord $PR$ from $E$ and $A$ respectively, so:
$$\boxed{A, P, E, R \text{ are concyclic.}} \tag{4}$$

---

**Step 2: $Q, E, C, R$ are concyclic.**

From the same tangent–chord angle (1): $\angle QER = \angle EDR = \angle BDR$. Since $B, D, R, C \in \odot(O)$, the angles $\angle BDR$ and $\angle BCR$ subtend the same arc $BR$:
$$\angle BDR = \angle BCR. \tag{5}$$
Since $B, Q, C$ are collinear, $\angle BCR = \angle QCR$. Therefore:
$$\angle QER = \angle QCR.$$
These are directed angles subtending chord $QR$ from $E$ and $C$, so:
$$\boxed{Q, E, C, R \text{ are concyclic.}} \tag{6}$$

---

**Step 3: $B, P, Q, R$ are concyclic.**

From (4) ($A, P, E, R$ concyclic), the angles $\angle APR$ and $\angle AER$ subtend the same arc $AR$:
$$\angle APR = \angle AER. \tag{7}$$

From (6) ($Q, E, C, R$ concyclic), the angles $\angle CQR$ and $\angle CER$ subtend the same arc $CR$:
$$\angle CQR = \angle CER. \tag{8}$$

Since $A, E, C$ are collinear (diagonal $AC$), $\angle AER = \angle CER$ (mod $\pi$). Combining with (7) and (8):
$$\angle APR = \angle CQR. \tag{9}$$

Since $A, P, B$ are collinear: $\angle BPR = \angle APR$.
Since $B, Q, C$ are collinear: $\angle BQR = \angle CQR$.

Substituting into (9):
$$\angle BPR = \angle BQR.$$

These are directed angles subtending segment $BR$ from $P$ and $Q$ respectively. Equality of these directed angles (mod $\pi$) means $P$ and $Q$ lie on the same circle through $B$ and $R$. Therefore:

$$\boxed{B, P, Q, R \text{ are concyclic.}} \qquad \blacksquare$$

